Were you running the full sample of SNPs in parallel? That would explain it since parallel processing chunks the runs across cores, and if certain runs within a core finish-up before your memory issues kick in you would end up with a somewhat random assortment of SNPs in your output. Still not something I've personally seen happen, but again you may have a specific cluster environment that is operating differently in terms of how it handles memory issues. If you weren't running in parallel we may need to do some more digging. Might be worth also specifically picking out 1000 snps that were not in in the output of 3.2 million SNPs to make sure that selected subset runs ok...

