Hi Helen,

Time and memory really depend on the model, number of traits being included, and number of SNPs left after running the sumstats function. In general, my experience with running a full set of SNPs in serial is that it will take upwards of 2 days, which is why we make running in parallel the package default. We are actively working on different ways to reduce the memory burden of the GWAS functions (which is why I combined the addSNPs and GWAS functions a few weeks ago), but in the meantime I would suggest running a subset of 5K SNPs to get a sense of run time and memory issues.

Since all runs are entirely independent of one another, and it sounds like you have access to computing clusters, I think your best bet would be to still run your analyses in parallel, but chunk the output from sumstats to subsets of SNPS and submit multiple jobs to avoid the memory issues. Hope this helps but happy to work further with you to find the best solution for your particular model and computing environment!

Best,
  Andrew

